{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYcoc9CneQu5xtRqKo4Cio","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-26; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: D1 \"64\" base64; D2 \"half a turn\" ROT13; D3\n8\t  \"sixteen fingers\" hex; D4 \"two sides\" 8-bit binary; D5 \"reduced to numbers\" decimal codes; D6 \"eight fingers\" octal;\n9\t  D7 \"Percent\" URL `%xx`; D8 \"Entity\" HTML `&#N;`; D9 \"Escape Room\" JSON `\"\\uXXXX\"`; D10 \"Thirty-Two Below\" base32;\n10\t  D11 \"URL-friendly\" base64url, `=` dropped; D12 \"wrong end\" REVERSED; D13 \"A met Z\" Atbash; D14 \"Caesar took a week\n11\t  off\" +7; D15 \"Forty-Seven\" ROT47; D16 \"Sixteen ... Dot dot dot\" MORSE over HEX; D17 \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's\n12\t  and b's\" BACON 5-groups = hex nibbles; D18 \"Three rails ... zigzag\" RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0; D19 \"Two bytes ... the\n13\t  little one goes first\" hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe`; D20 \"Same bar ... the big one\" UTF-16BE + `feff`; D21 \"A familiar\n14\t  wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" GZIP in base64 (`H4sI`); D22 \"Another familiar wrapper ... inflation\" ZLIB\n15\t  `78da` in base64 (`eNo`); D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe ... neighbors\" ASCII85 `<~...~>`; D24 \"Count\n16\t  carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\" NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...`; D25 \"Every character has a\n17\t  point. A code point, anyway.\"/\"You Have a Point\" Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...` (D9's idea in U+ notation);\n18\t  D26 \"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\"/\"Food for Thought\" (= brain food) BRAINFUCK: a PROGRAM whose output\n19\t  is the sentence (only `+ - .`, one cell, no loops).\n20\t  D1-D15 and D23-D26 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two, the pun naming BOTH (every number/word may be a layer or a key;\n21\t  \"familiar\" = a seen codec as the OUTER layer). D20/D22 twisted ONE PARAMETER of the previous day (\"Same\"/\"Another\").\n22\t  Families: D1-D11 base-N/char codes/escapes, D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18\n23\t  transposition, D19-D20 byte representation, D21-D22 compression, D23 bigger base-N alphabet, D24 framing, D25\n24\t  character notation, D26 ESOTERIC LANGUAGE (execute, don't decode). D23-D26 = four new families in a row; only a\n25\t  \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt signals a knob day. On an unknown shape read the blob's STRUCTURE (repeating unit,\n26\t  token shape, a tiny alphabet of operators) and name the spec from it + the pun (\"language\" = a program to RUN).\n27\t- Mastery is cumulative: day N = 5N-2 sets x N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, line k = day k's encoding (D26 line =\n28\t  a 166-217 B BF program printing a 5-char tail; 128 x 26). Last line has no trailing newline; fragments split\n29\t  mid-word and join verbatim.\n30\t- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s / Mastery ~5-9 s windows; since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18: 30 s / 105 s). The HARNESS\n31\t  kills solve.py at 60 s regardless (D17, D21). Recent: D22 Mastery 108 x 22 solve 15.9 s; D23 TT 5.1 s, Mastery first\n32\t  run INCORRECT (all NN wrong), re-submitted on the SAME attempt (solve 1.2 s); D24 TT 4.6 s / Mastery 4.5 s first try;\n33\t  D25 TT 1.1 s first try, Mastery 123 x 25 first submit INCORRECT (all NN wrong, the D23 bug class again), fixed and\n34\t  re-submitted on the same attempt: 2.1 s (solve 0.76 s). D26 TT 3.9 s (solve 0.44 s) first try; Mastery 128 x 26\n35\t  solver_incomplete (solve 64.9 s, 0 answers, harness kill) -> a LIVE worker wrote an ad-hoc 26-codec positional decoder\n36\t  and submitted CORRECT ~2 min 20 s after start; the generalize workers were cut off before a fix landed.\n37\t\n38\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n39\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n40\t  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n41\t  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n42\t  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n43\t  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n44\t  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n45\t- **Code-point notation (D9, D25)** -- the same \"one token per char\" idea in different dress. Values seen: D9 JSON\n46\t  `\"\\u00XX\"` (quoted, lowercase); D25 single/TT `U+XXXX` UPPERCASE hex, exactly 4 digits, ONE space between tokens, no\n47\t  newline (762 B = 7 B per char; spaces are `U+0020`, so no literal space is payload); Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per\n48\t  fragment (`U+006C U+0065 U+002D U+0035 U+0032`), 123/123 sets. Parse rule (D25 `_uplus`, lib\n49\t  `warmups_codecs.uplus_dec`): the WHOLE text must be tokens `(?:U\\+|u\\+|\\\\u|\\\\U|%u|0x)[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8}` split by\n50\t  whitespace/`,;` -> `chr(int(h,16))` (2-8 digits covers astral `U+1F600`, `\\U0001F600`); anything else -> None.\n51\t  Unbuilt knobs: braces `\\u{4E}`/`\\x{4E}`, decimal/octal code points, names, offset blocks (atlas gap list).\n52\t- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n53\t  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n54\t  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n55\t- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n56\t  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n57\t  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n58\t  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n59\t  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n60\t- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n61\t  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n62\t  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n63\t  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n64\t  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n65\t  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n66\t  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n67\t- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n68\t  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n69\t  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n70\t  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n71\t  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n72\t  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n73\t  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n74\t  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n75\t- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n76\t  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n77\t  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n78\t  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n79\t  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n80\t  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n81\t  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n82\t  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n83\t  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n84\t- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n85\t  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n86\t  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n87\t  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n88\t  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n89\t  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n90\t- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n91\t  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n92\t  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n93\t  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n94\t  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n95\t  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n96\t  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n97\t  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n98\t  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n99\t  decoder on the inflated text.\n100\t- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n101\t  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n102\t  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n103\t  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n104\t  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n105\t- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,` concatenated, no\n106\t  separators or newline, EVERY len = `1` (444 B for ~111 chars = 4 B per char); the payload's own `,` and `:` appear\n107\t  as data (`1:,,`, `1::,`), and spaces are data (`1: ,`). Mastery line 24 = the same 1-char netstrings over the fragment\n108\t  (`1:o,1:n,1:-,1:3,1:7,`, trailing `,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (D24 solver `_netstr`): cursor loop --\n109\t  `\\s*(\\d+):` at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end) right after, advance; accept only if the WHOLE text\n110\t  parses (else None, so plain text never matches); `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, fn)` for the Mastery line. Never\n111\t  `split(',')`. The loop already covers len > 1 and mixed lengths; unbuilt knobs: len in UTF-8 BYTES vs chars,\n112\t  hex/varint/binary length prefixes, tnetstrings (type tag `,#^!~]}` instead of `,`), bencode (`4:spam`, `l..e`),\n113\t  RESP (`$5\\r\\nhello\\r\\n`), HTTP chunked, `index:char` frames OUT OF ORDER, `count:char` run-length, nested frames.\n114\t- **Esoteric program dialect (D26)** -- the blob is a PROGRAM; its output is the text. Values seen: single/TT = ONE line\n115\t  of Brainfuck, alphabet exactly `+ - .` (no `<>[],`, no comments, no newline), ONE cell from 0, each char = a run of\n116\t  ONLY `+` or ONLY `-` equal to the difference from the previous char, then `.`; never wraps past 0/255 (3406 B for 109\n117\t  chars, ~31 B per char); Mastery line 26 = the same over a 5-char slug tail, restarting at cell 0 (166-217 B, 128/128\n118\t  sets). Parse rule (D26 solver `_bf`): whole text in `[+\\-<>\\[\\].,\\s]` with at least one `.` -> a full interpreter\n119\t  (8-bit wrapping cells, bracket map, `,` reads 0, step cap) -> output, printable gate. The interpreter already\n120\t  covers loops/`<>`; unbuilt knobs: comment text between commands, token-substituted dialects (Ook!, Blub, COW,\n121\t  Pikalang, ReverseFuck), other tiny languages (Deadfish, Whitespace, Befunge, JSFuck) -- atlas gap list.\n122\t- **Line wrapper / positional routing** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in\n123\t  number order. Line k is decoded by `decode._positional` with day k's REGISTERED codec; a line whose day has no\n124\t  registered codec goes to `_unknown_line_cands`, which re-\"decodes\" text that is already plain (D25: `le-52` -> `-07`).\n125\t  `_positional` checks `enc(dec(line)) == line` for a `warmups_codecs.DAYS` entry: D26's solver appended\n126\t  `(26, \"brainfuck\", enc, _bf)` with enc = `'+'*ord(c)+'.>'` (absolute value per cell) while the GM writes one-cell\n127\t  DELTAS -> the re-encode check failed on every set -> last line sent to `_unknown_line_cands` -> no template hit -> the\n128\t  joint path -> 64.9 s timeout. Rule: every new day = `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line (no re-encode\n129\t  check), or a DAYS entry whose ENCODER reproduces the GM's exact bytes (assert it on one real Mastery line).\n130\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24, 123 x 25 on day 25, 128 x 26 on day 26); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n131\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n132\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n133\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n134\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n135\t\n136\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n137\t| What you see | First move |\n138\t|---|---|\n139\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n140\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n141\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n142\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n143\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n144\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n145\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n146\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n147\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n148\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n149\t| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n150\t| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n151\t| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n152\t| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n153\t| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n154\t| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n155\t| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n156\t| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n157\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n158\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n159\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n160\t| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n161\t| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n162\t| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n163\t| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n164\t| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> the D26 solver's `_bf` interpreter. `Ook. Ook?` / `Blub.` / `MoO` -> map tokens to BF first. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n165\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n166\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n167\t\n168\t## Day by day\n169\t**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n170\t`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\n171\tPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\n172\trot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\n173\tWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\n174\trefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n175\t\n176\t**Days 14-25** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n177\t+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n178\t`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points): single CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in\n179\tPitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs `register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a\n180\tmiddle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to\n181\tit; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC\n182\trule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc solver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line\n183\t(D25: no day-25 codec registered -> `_unknown_line_cands` re-decoded plain `le-52` to `le-07`; fixed with\n184\t`register_free(\"uplus\", 25, ...)` on the RAW line); D24 a strict whole-text cursor parser (None on mismatch) sits first\n185\tin the pipeline -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue.\n186\t\n187\t**Day 26: Food for Thought** (\"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\" -- BRAINFUCK, \"brain food\").\n188\t- 3406-byte `blob.txt`, one line, no newline, alphabet exactly `+ - .`: `+`x78 `.` `+`x27 `.` `-`x6 `.` ... -- one\n189\t  cell, each character = the signed difference from the previous one as a run of one sign, then `.` (109 dots = 109\n190\t  chars). One layer, no loops, no `<>`. Pun: \"very small language\" = an 8-command esolang, \"a lot to say\" = output;\n191\t  \"Food for Thought\" = brain food. Triage previewed the `+++.` soup; nothing in lib (atlas row 83 \"Brainfuck/Ook!\", rated\n192\t  LOW: \"program, not encoding\" -- wrong). Worker wrote a 30-line step-capped interpreter `_bf` (no pip, no node).\n193\t- Single: first submit, start 13:00:06.1Z -> CORRECT 13:00:32.7Z (~27 s); slug accepted exactly as printed.\n194\t- Solver: D25 lane solver + whole-blob `_bf` step (gated: whole text in `[+\\-<>\\[\\].,\\s]`, has `.`) + a runtime\n195\t  `warmups_codecs.DAYS` entry for day 26. TT CORRECT 3.9 s first try. Mastery 128 x 26: solve 64.9 s, ZERO answers\n196\t  (harness kill): the DAYS entry's encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) does not reproduce the GM's delta bytes, so\n197\t  `_positional`'s re-encode check rejected line 26 on every set and each set fell through `_unknown_line_cands` into\n198\t  the joint search (diagnosis by code reading -- the two generalize workers profiled but were cut off at CORRECT; the\n199\t  solver in `solver/` still carries the bug). Live worker: ad-hoc positional decoder for all 26 codecs -> CORRECT.\n200\t- Lesson: a runtime codec registration is only as good as its ENCODER when `_positional` round-trips it. Prefer\n201\t  `register_free(\"bf\", 26, ...)` (decode-only) or encode in the GM's dialect, and time `hctf solver-test` on a REAL\n202\t  Mastery case before any timed start (no D26 Mastery case existed before the run). Lane rating: esoteric languages\n203\t  are now a live family -- a program blob means INTERPRET (pure python, step cap), never decode by charset tables.\n204\t\n205\t## GM answer conventions learned\n206\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n207\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-26; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n208\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n209\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n210\t\n211\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n212\t- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n213\t  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n214\t  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n215\t- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n216\t  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n217\t  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n218\t  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n219\t  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n220\t- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n221\t  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n222\t  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n223\t- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23 113/113 and D25 123/123 sets wrong once each): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n224\t  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n225\t  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n226\t  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n227\t  D25: a day with NO registered codec makes `_positional` send its (pre-decoded, plain) line to `_unknown_line_cands`,\n228\t  which mangles it -- register the codec for today's day number even if the whole-blob path already decodes it.\n229\t  D26: a REGISTERED `DAYS` codec whose encoder doesn't round-trip the GM bytes is as bad as none (and slower: every set\n230\t  pays the unknown-line + joint search -> 60 s kill). Many-to-one codecs (programs, padding, case-free, whitespace\n231\t  variants) can't be round-tripped -> use `register_free` (decode-only) for them.\n232\t- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n233\t  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n234\t  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n235\t  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n236\t  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n237\t- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n238\t  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n239\t- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n240\t  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n241\t- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n242\t  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n243\t  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n244\t  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n245\t- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n246\t  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n247\t- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n248\t  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n249\t  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n250\t  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n251\t- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n252\t  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n253\t  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n254\t- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n255\t  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n256\t  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n257\t- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n258\t  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n259\t  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n260\t- PROGRAM blobs (D26 Brainfuck): interpret in a pure-python, step-capped interpreter (8-bit wrapping cells, `,` = 0);\n261\t  never hand them to node/eval/an online runner. A loop-free one-cell BF is just a running sum of `+`/`-` read at `.`.\n262\t  A BF charset test also matches Morse (`.`/`-`/space, D16 line 16): gate the interpreter's OUTPUT (printable + slug),\n263\t  never the charset alone.\n264\t- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n265\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n266\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n267\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20e4578b-af83-47db-a5d2-12b070c2e725","uuid":"9d806122-35be-44a3-9bd8-88341c412a3c","timestamp":"2026-10-27T13:06:26.625Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-26; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: D1 \"64\" base64; D2 \"half a turn\" ROT13; D3\n  \"sixteen fingers\" hex; D4 \"two sides\" 8-bit binary; D5 \"reduced to numbers\" decimal codes; D6 \"eight fingers\" octal;\n  D7 \"Percent\" URL `%xx`; D8 \"Entity\" HTML `&#N;`; D9 \"Escape Room\" JSON `\"\\uXXXX\"`; D10 \"Thirty-Two Below\" base32;\n  D11 \"URL-friendly\" base64url, `=` dropped; D12 \"wrong end\" REVERSED; D13 \"A met Z\" Atbash; D14 \"Caesar took a week\n  off\" +7; D15 \"Forty-Seven\" ROT47; D16 \"Sixteen ... Dot dot dot\" MORSE over HEX; D17 \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's\n  and b's\" BACON 5-groups = hex nibbles; D18 \"Three rails ... zigzag\" RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0; D19 \"Two bytes ... the\n  little one goes first\" hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe`; D20 \"Same bar ... the big one\" UTF-16BE + `feff`; D21 \"A familiar\n  wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" GZIP in base64 (`H4sI`); D22 \"Another familiar wrapper ... inflation\" ZLIB\n  `78da` in base64 (`eNo`); D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe ... neighbors\" ASCII85 `<~...~>`; D24 \"Count\n  carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\" NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...`; D25 \"Every character has a\n  point. A code point, anyway.\"/\"You Have a Point\" Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...` (D9's idea in U+ notation);\n  D26 \"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\"/\"Food for Thought\" (= brain food) BRAINFUCK: a PROGRAM whose output\n  is the sentence (only `+ - .`, one cell, no loops).\n  D1-D15 and D23-D26 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D22 two, the pun naming BOTH (every number/word may be a layer or a key;\n  \"familiar\" = a seen codec as the OUTER layer). D20/D22 twisted ONE PARAMETER of the previous day (\"Same\"/\"Another\").\n  Families: D1-D11 base-N/char codes/escapes, D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18\n  transposition, D19-D20 byte representation, D21-D22 compression, D23 bigger base-N alphabet, D24 framing, D25\n  character notation, D26 ESOTERIC LANGUAGE (execute, don't decode). D23-D26 = four new families in a row; only a\n  \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt signals a knob day. On an unknown shape read the blob's STRUCTURE (repeating unit,\n  token shape, a tiny alphabet of operators) and name the spec from it + the pun (\"language\" = a program to RUN).\n- Mastery is cumulative: day N = 5N-2 sets x N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, line k = day k's encoding (D26 line =\n  a 166-217 B BF program printing a 5-char tail; 128 x 26). Last line has no trailing newline; fragments split\n  mid-word and join verbatim.\n- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s / Mastery ~5-9 s windows; since D16 mostly `deadline: null` (D18: 30 s / 105 s). The HARNESS\n  kills solve.py at 60 s regardless (D17, D21). Recent: D22 Mastery 108 x 22 solve 15.9 s; D23 TT 5.1 s, Mastery first\n  run INCORRECT (all NN wrong), re-submitted on the SAME attempt (solve 1.2 s); D24 TT 4.6 s / Mastery 4.5 s first try;\n  D25 TT 1.1 s first try, Mastery 123 x 25 first submit INCORRECT (all NN wrong, the D23 bug class again), fixed and\n  re-submitted on the same attempt: 2.1 s (solve 0.76 s). D26 TT 3.9 s (solve 0.44 s) first try; Mastery 128 x 26\n  solver_incomplete (solve 64.9 s, 0 answers, harness kill) -> a LIVE worker wrote an ad-hoc 26-codec positional decoder\n  and submitted CORRECT ~2 min 20 s after start; the generalize workers were cut off before a fix landed.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n- **Code-point notation (D9, D25)** -- the same \"one token per char\" idea in different dress. Values seen: D9 JSON\n  `\"\\u00XX\"` (quoted, lowercase); D25 single/TT `U+XXXX` UPPERCASE hex, exactly 4 digits, ONE space between tokens, no\n  newline (762 B = 7 B per char; spaces are `U+0020`, so no literal space is payload); Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per\n  fragment (`U+006C U+0065 U+002D U+0035 U+0032`), 123/123 sets. Parse rule (D25 `_uplus`, lib\n  `warmups_codecs.uplus_dec`): the WHOLE text must be tokens `(?:U\\+|u\\+|\\\\u|\\\\U|%u|0x)[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8}` split by\n  whitespace/`,;` -> `chr(int(h,16))` (2-8 digits covers astral `U+1F600`, `\\U0001F600`); anything else -> None.\n  Unbuilt knobs: braces `\\u{4E}`/`\\x{4E}`, decimal/octal code points, names, offset blocks (atlas gap list).\n- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13-D15)** -- several FREE-TEXT transforms coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n  reversal (12), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; not letters-only: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`/`` ` ``/`a`-`h`).\n  Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on\n  20/63 sets. Parse rule (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`): decode the strict lines, then try every assignment\n  of the free codecs to the free lines, EACH USED ONCE per blob, keep the one whose joined text ends in a full 3-word\n  `adj-noun-noun-NN` slug (rank 3-word above 2-word: D14 set22 `g-voaafk|urtle-60`), best bigram. Any new keyless\n  printable-range shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a case/charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text. D16\n  Morse: symbols split by ONE space, no word gaps, decoded symbols ONLY `0-9A-F` (UPPERCASE; `fromhex` takes it), ~11 B\n  per char; Mastery line 16 = 14 symbols = 7 bytes. D17 Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb`\n  (values 0-15, every group starts `a`), two groups per byte; read as Bacon-26 letters it gives only `a`-`p` (a middle\n  layer). Parse rule: after any carrier decode, output `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` or exactly 16 symbols -> nibbles ->\n  `fromhex`; digits-only -> char codes; else `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug.\n  For any two-symbol group carrier: map to 0/1, fixed-width groups, values < 16 -> hex nibbles, < 26 -> Bacon letters\n  (`warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`, widths 4-8, both polarities). In lib: `decode_chunk` Morse-hex and BACON-HEX branches.\n- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- D21 single/TT = ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member\n  (`1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03` = b64 `H4sIAAAAAAACA`); D22 = ONE std padded base64 string of a ZLIB stream `78da` -> b64\n  `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNq`/`eNr` (3rd char follows the first deflate byte: match `eN`). Mastery lines 21/22 = 36- / 20-char\n  padded fragments, each its OWN gzip member / zlib stream, side by side. Payload plain UTF-8; compressed bytes are not\n  printable, so printable-gated b64 branches reject them and rot-N returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, took D22\n  unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode -> sniff (`1f8b`, zlib, `BZh`,\n  `fd377a585a`) -> decompress -> UTF-8; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n  Widen the zlib test to RFC 1950 (`b[0]&15==8`, `(b[0]<<8|b[1])%31==0`, FDICT -> `zdict=` crib) and try raw deflate\n  (`wbits=-15`); same rule for any wrapper (hex/b32/b85) and compressor (lzma-alone, zstd, lz4); re-run the full\n  decoder on the inflated text.\n- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,` concatenated, no\n  separators or newline, EVERY len = `1` (444 B for ~111 chars = 4 B per char); the payload's own `,` and `:` appear\n  as data (`1:,,`, `1::,`), and spaces are data (`1: ,`). Mastery line 24 = the same 1-char netstrings over the fragment\n  (`1:o,1:n,1:-,1:3,1:7,`, trailing `,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (D24 solver `_netstr`): cursor loop --\n  `\\s*(\\d+):` at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end) right after, advance; accept only if the WHOLE text\n  parses (else None, so plain text never matches); `register_free(\"netstr\", 24, fn)` for the Mastery line. Never\n  `split(',')`. The loop already covers len > 1 and mixed lengths; unbuilt knobs: len in UTF-8 BYTES vs chars,\n  hex/varint/binary length prefixes, tnetstrings (type tag `,#^!~]}` instead of `,`), bencode (`4:spam`, `l..e`),\n  RESP (`$5\\r\\nhello\\r\\n`), HTTP chunked, `index:char` frames OUT OF ORDER, `count:char` run-length, nested frames.\n- **Esoteric program dialect (D26)** -- the blob is a PROGRAM; its output is the text. Values seen: single/TT = ONE line\n  of Brainfuck, alphabet exactly `+ - .` (no `<>[],`, no comments, no newline), ONE cell from 0, each char = a run of\n  ONLY `+` or ONLY `-` equal to the difference from the previous char, then `.`; never wraps past 0/255 (3406 B for 109\n  chars, ~31 B per char); Mastery line 26 = the same over a 5-char slug tail, restarting at cell 0 (166-217 B, 128/128\n  sets). Parse rule (D26 solver `_bf`): whole text in `[+\\-<>\\[\\].,\\s]` with at least one `.` -> a full interpreter\n  (8-bit wrapping cells, bracket map, `,` reads 0, step cap) -> output, printable gate. The interpreter already\n  covers loops/`<>`; unbuilt knobs: comment text between commands, token-substituted dialects (Ook!, Blub, COW,\n  Pikalang, ReverseFuck), other tiny languages (Deadfish, Whitespace, Befunge, JSFuck) -- atlas gap list.\n- **Line wrapper / positional routing** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in\n  number order. Line k is decoded by `decode._positional` with day k's REGISTERED codec; a line whose day has no\n  registered codec goes to `_unknown_line_cands`, which re-\"decodes\" text that is already plain (D25: `le-52` -> `-07`).\n  `_positional` checks `enc(dec(line)) == line` for a `warmups_codecs.DAYS` entry: D26's solver appended\n  `(26, \"brainfuck\", enc, _bf)` with enc = `'+'*ord(c)+'.>'` (absolute value per cell) while the GM writes one-cell\n  DELTAS -> the re-encode check failed on every set -> last line sent to `_unknown_line_cands` -> no template hit -> the\n  joint path -> 64.9 s timeout. Rule: every new day = `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line (no re-encode\n  check), or a DAYS entry whose ENCODER reproduces the GM's exact bytes (assert it on one real Mastery line).\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, ..., 108 x 22 on day 22, 113 x 23 on day 23, 118 x 24 on day 24, 123 x 25 on day 25, 128 x 26 on day 26); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> the D26 solver's `_bf` interpreter. `Ook. Ook?` / `Blub.` / `MoO` -> map tokens to BF first. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\nrot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\nWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\nrefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n\n**Days 14-25** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points): single CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs `register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a\nmiddle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's `rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to\nit; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's magic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC\nrule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc solver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line\n(D25: no day-25 codec registered -> `_unknown_line_cands` re-decoded plain `le-52` to `le-07`; fixed with\n`register_free(\"uplus\", 25, ...)` on the RAW line); D24 a strict whole-text cursor parser (None on mismatch) sits first\nin the pipeline -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue.\n\n**Day 26: Food for Thought** (\"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\" -- BRAINFUCK, \"brain food\").\n- 3406-byte `blob.txt`, one line, no newline, alphabet exactly `+ - .`: `+`x78 `.` `+`x27 `.` `-`x6 `.` ... -- one\n  cell, each character = the signed difference from the previous one as a run of one sign, then `.` (109 dots = 109\n  chars). One layer, no loops, no `<>`. Pun: \"very small language\" = an 8-command esolang, \"a lot to say\" = output;\n  \"Food for Thought\" = brain food. Triage previewed the `+++.` soup; nothing in lib (atlas row 83 \"Brainfuck/Ook!\", rated\n  LOW: \"program, not encoding\" -- wrong). Worker wrote a 30-line step-capped interpreter `_bf` (no pip, no node).\n- Single: first submit, start 13:00:06.1Z -> CORRECT 13:00:32.7Z (~27 s); slug accepted exactly as printed.\n- Solver: D25 lane solver + whole-blob `_bf` step (gated: whole text in `[+\\-<>\\[\\].,\\s]`, has `.`) + a runtime\n  `warmups_codecs.DAYS` entry for day 26. TT CORRECT 3.9 s first try. Mastery 128 x 26: solve 64.9 s, ZERO answers\n  (harness kill): the DAYS entry's encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) does not reproduce the GM's delta bytes, so\n  `_positional`'s re-encode check rejected line 26 on every set and each set fell through `_unknown_line_cands` into\n  the joint search (diagnosis by code reading -- the two generalize workers profiled but were cut off at CORRECT; the\n  solver in `solver/` still carries the bug). Live worker: ad-hoc positional decoder for all 26 codecs -> CORRECT.\n- Lesson: a runtime codec registration is only as good as its ENCODER when `_positional` round-trips it. Prefer\n  `register_free(\"bf\", 26, ...)` (decode-only) or encode in the GM's dialect, and time `hctf solver-test` on a REAL\n  Mastery case before any timed start (no D26 Mastery case existed before the run). Lane rating: esoteric languages\n  are now a live family -- a program blob means INTERPRET (pure python, step cap), never decode by charset tables.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-26; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n- PRE-DECODING MASTERY LINES (D23 113/113 and D25 123/123 sets wrong once each): never rewrite a numbered line in place to plaintext before\n  `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` -- the joint search has no \"identity\" choice, so a plain line gets a leftover free codec\n  (digit ROT5 turned `el-46` into `el-91`). Register the codec instead and let it claim the RAW line. Check the joined\n  Mastery sentence of one set by eye before a timed start; all-sets-wrong-in-the-same-field = one line's routing.\n  D25: a day with NO registered codec makes `_positional` send its (pre-decoded, plain) line to `_unknown_line_cands`,\n  which mangles it -- register the codec for today's day number even if the whole-blob path already decodes it.\n  D26: a REGISTERED `DAYS` codec whose encoder doesn't round-trip the GM bytes is as bad as none (and slower: every set\n  pays the unknown-line + joint search -> 60 s kill). Many-to-one codecs (programs, padding, case-free, whitespace\n  variants) can't be round-tripped -> use `register_free` (decode-only) for them.\n- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n- PROGRAM blobs (D26 Brainfuck): interpret in a pure-python, step-capped interpreter (8-bit wrapping cells, `,` = 0);\n  never hand them to node/eval/an online runner. A loop-free one-cell BF is just a running sum of `+`/`-` read at `.`.\n  A BF charset test also matches Morse (`.`/`-`/space, D16 line 16): gate the interpreter's OUTPUT (printable + slug),\n  never the charset alone.\n- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n","numLines":267,"startLine":1,"totalLines":267}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TYcoc9CneQu5xtRqKo4Cio","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
