: d=ord(c)-v; out+=('+'*d if d>0 else '-'*-d)+'.'; v=ord(c)), then hctf solver-test the variant-mastery-ca7796e4 case (128 x 26) and check < 5 s.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"rhythm-broken-structure-first\", \"concept\": \"when the day is a NEW family with no charset fingerprint, read the blob's structure first\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D24 broke the D19-D23 'new family, then one knob' rhythm (no Ascii85 knob; framing instead), and nothing prebuilt covered it. Worker rule: if STEP 1 (lib) misses, describe the repeating unit (here '\\\\d+:.,' x 111) and name the spec from it + the title pun before brute-forcing codecs; write a strict whole-text parser that returns None on mismatch so it can sit first in the pipeline. D25 confirmed the pattern (third new family in a row: code points, a D9 sibling); the prebuilt row-22 decoder caught it, so keep the queue wide across families. D26 made it four new families in a row and the prompt named a LANGUAGE: a blob with a tiny operator alphabet (`+ - .`) and run structure is a PROGRAM -> interpret it (pure python, step cap), don't table-decode.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mastery-line-routing\", \"concept\": \"Mastery: every day's codec must claim its RAW line inside decode_chunked / _joint_free\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D23 Mastery lost its first submit (113/113 sets: right slug words, wrong NN, every digit +5): the solver pre-decoded line 23 ('<~..~>' -> 'el-46') in place, and the joint free-codec search -- which has no identity option -- gave the plain line a digit ROT5. Rule: never rewrite numbered lines before decode_chunked; add the day's codec via register_free(name, day, fn) (gated to its own charset/delimiters) or decode_chunk. Before any timed start, print one set's joined sentence and check the slug digits against the single-solve shape. Same fields wrong on every set = one line's routing. D24 confirmed the rule: _netstr registered as register_free('netstr', 24, ...) and skipped as a whole-blob step for numbered blobs -> Mastery 118 x 24 first try. D25 REPEATED the bug through another path (123/123 sets NN wrong): _uplus_lines pre-decoded line 25 and, with no day-25 codec registered, decode._positional sent the plain line to _unknown_line_cands, which re-decoded it. Checklist for every new day: (1) register_free(name, day, fn) for TODAY's day number; (2) the whole-blob step must skip numbered Mastery blobs; (3) print one set's joined sentence and compare NN with the raw last line before submitting. D26 broke it a THIRD way: the solver appended a runtime warmups_codecs.DAYS entry (26, 'brainfuck', enc='+'*ord(c)+'.>', _bf) whose encoder does not reproduce the GM's one-cell DELTA bytes; _positional's re-encode check rejected line 26 on all 128 sets -> _unknown_line_cands + joint search per set -> solve 64.9 s, 0 answers (harness kill); a live worker hand-decoded and submitted. (4) A DAYS entry must round-trip a REAL Mastery line (assert enc(dec(line)) == line) -- many-to-one codecs (programs, whitespace/padding variants) go through register_free instead; (5) time hctf solver-test on a real Mastery case before the timed start.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"byte-family-mastery-cost\", \"concept\": \"Mastery solve time per set as codecs accumulate; lane-solver continuity\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D21 Mastery (103 x 21) died at the 60 s harness budget because solve.py was an ad-hoc generic per-line brute force, not the carried D20 lane solver; the D20 solver + one `_b64z` branch did 0.34 s. D22 Mastery (108 x 22) passed first try with the D21 solver unchanged (plus the solverrt partial-emit wrapper) but solve took 15.9 s and submit 11.8 s -- profile per set if it grows; D23 (113 x 23) solved in 1.2 s. Rule: copy the previous day's solve.py, add today's codec as one whole-blob step + register_free(name, day, fn), return early, time hctf solver-test on the real Mastery case before any timed start. D24 (118 x 24): solve 1.6 s, whole Mastery 4.5 s. D25 (123 x 25): solve 0.76 s.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bf-token-dialects\", \"concept\": \"Brainfuck token dialects: Ook!, Blub, COW, Pikalang, ReverseFuck, Alphuck, Roadrunner, Binaryfuck/3-bit, custom 8-token maps\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Most likely D27 knob ('same language, other words'; Ook! is the canonical BF sibling). bf_dialect_dec(t): Ook (tokens 'Ook.'/'Ook?'/'Ook!' paired: '. ?'='>' '? .'='<' '. .'='+' '! !'='-' '! .'='.' '. !'=',' '! ?'='[' '? !'=']'; also short Ook '.?' pairs without the word), Blub (same pairing), COW (moo mOo moO mOO Moo MOo MoO MOO OOO MMM OOM oom; MoO=+, MOo=-, moO=>, mOo=<, Moo=out-or-in, MOO/moo loops), Pikalang (pipi=>, pichu=<, pi=+, ka=-, pikachu=., pikapi=,, pika=[, chu=]), ReverseFuck (+/- and </> swapped), Alphuck (a c e i j o p s), Binaryfuck/3-bit (000..111 per command), Roadrunner (Meep variants). Then the D26 _bf interpreter. Unknown token set of <= 8 distinct words with run structure: assign tokens to +-.<>[], by the crib (the program must print 'Nice work') -- brute 8! only over tokens seen. Register in CANDIDATES + decode_chunk + register_free for 5-char Mastery fragments. Fixtures: each dialect encoding the sentence with the GM's D26 delta style AND with loops; decoys (English prose, Morse).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bf-full-programs\", \"concept\": \"full Brainfuck: loops/multiplication generators, many cells, comment text between commands, wrap below 0, ',' input, 16-bit cells, BF under a seen wrapper\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Knob on D26 (only + - . seen). Move the D26 solver's _bf into lib.warmups_codecs.bf_run(prog, steps=10**7, cells=65536, bits=8) + bf_dec(t): accept when, after removing non-command chars, the program has balanced brackets and >= 1 '.', run it, printable + slug gate; try 8-bit wrap first, then 16-bit/unbounded cells if output is junk; ',' reads 0 (or EOF=-1 variant). Also peel: if a base64/hex/gzip decode yields BF soup, run it (stack with decompress-then-redecode). Encoders for fixtures: D26 delta style, multiplication-loop generator (n = a*b + c per char), cell-per-char with '>' and a shared 10x loop (the common 'bf text generator' output). Decoys: Morse lines (. - space) must not be claimed unless the program output passes the slug gate.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"deadfish\", \"concept\": \"Deadfish (i d s o; value reset to 0 at 256 or -1) and the XKCD variant (x k c d)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'A very small language' read literally (4 commands, output = the number, or chr(number) in the char variant). deadfish_dec(t): only [idso] (or [xkcd]) + whitespace; v=0; i +1, d -1, s v*v, o -> chr(v) (char-output variant, what a text-printing challenge needs; also collect numbers and try chr); after each op, v in {-1, 256} -> 0. Also accept uppercase. Fixtures: delta style (i/d runs then o) and square-based style (s to jump), XKCD letters; decoys: English words made of i/d/s/o only ('dido') must fail the slug gate.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"whitespace-language\", \"concept\": \"Whitespace programming language (space/tab/LF; also visible S/T/L or [Space][Tab][LF] notation)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Esolang sibling of D26. ws_dec(t): keep only ' ', '\\\\t', '\\\\n' (or map S/T/L letters, [Space]/[Tab]/[LF] tokens); parse IMPs: SS push (sign S=+ T=-, binary S=0 T=1, L end), SLS dup, SLT swap, SLL drop, TSSS add, TSST sub, TSSL mul, TLSS output char, TLST output num, LSS label, LSL call, LLL end, etc.; run with a step cap; join output. Must sit BEFORE whitespace-stego (row 106) and zero-width decoders, and those must stay as fallbacks. Fixtures: push+print per char (typical generator output), with labels/loops, visible S/T/L notation; decoys: whitespace stego blobs (8-bit space/tab).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"befunge-2d\", \"concept\": \"Befunge-93/98 and ><> (2-D grid languages)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"befunge_dec(t): grid 80x25 (or text bounds), PC starts (0,0) moving right; implement 0-9 + - * / % ! ` > < ^ v ? (deterministic: treat ? as >) _ | \\\" : \\\\\\\\ $ . , # g p & ~ @ ; string mode pushes ords; ',' outputs chr; step cap 10^6. ><>: o outputs, ; ends, ' and \\\" strings, mirrors / \\\\\\\\ | _ #. Fixtures: the common '\\\"!dlrow olleH\\\">:#,_@' style generator for the sentence, multi-line v/< loops, ><> version; decoys: ASCII art (row 181).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"jsfuck-aaencode\", \"concept\": \"JSFuck ([]()!+ only), aaencode (Japanese emoticons), jjencode ($=~[] style) -- JS-from-tiny-alphabet encodings\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"NEVER node/eval (untrusted program). JSFuck: a pure-python evaluator for the JSFuck expression algebra (values: [], ![], +[], []+[], string indexing, [][\\\"flat\\\"] -> function source text constants, Function('return ...')() for the final string; implement the ~40 primitives the jsfuck.com encoder uses) and read the string passed to the final call (eval/Function body) -- usually alert(\\\"...\\\") or the bare string. aaencode/jjencode: known static decoders (regex the per-char octal/hex digit expressions, e.g. aadecode by replacing the emoticon digit symbols) -> the JS source -> extract the string literal. Fixtures from a vendored encoder (pure python port) for the sentence; decoys: base64/ROT47 soups.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"chef-recipe-lang\", \"concept\": \"Chef (recipe-shaped language) and Shakespeare (play-shaped) programs that print text\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Food for Thought' -> 'recipe' pun risk. chef_dec(t): parse 'Ingredients.' (amount [measure] name; amounts = char codes), 'Method.' steps: Put X into [the] [nth] mixing bowl (push), Fold, Add/Remove/Combine/Divide, Liquefy [contents of the] mixing bowl (mark as chars), Pour contents of the [nth] mixing bowl into the [nth] baking dish, 'Serves N.' (output dishes, top of stack first = REVERSED push order); loops 'Verb the ingredient.' ... 'Verb until verbed.' with a step cap. Shakespeare: subset (characters, 'You are as <adj> as <noun>' arithmetic = powers of 2, 'Speak your mind!' outputs chr). Fixtures: the common 'Hello World Souffle' style generator for the sentence; decoys: plain English recipes without Method.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"stack-vm-listing\", \"concept\": \"toy stack-VM / assembly / one-liner listings that print characters (PUSH 78 / OUT, Forth 78 emit, dc 78P, PostScript, mov al,0x4e; HQ9+)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'A very small language' read as a tiny instruction set. vm_dec(t): tokenise lines into mnemonic + int args (decimal/hex/char literal); simulate a generic machine: PUSH/LOAD/MOV/SET n, ADD/SUB/INC/DEC/MUL, OUT/PRINT/PUTC/EMIT/WRITE/P/INT 0x10 (print low byte or top of stack), JMP/LOOP only with a step cap; Forth 'n emit', dc 'nP' / 'n P', PostScript '(..) show'. Output gate: printable + slug. NEVER execute as real code. Fixtures: 4-5 synthetic dialects (stack, register, Forth, dc, 8086-ish) printing the sentence; decoys: decimal char-code lists (D5 must still win).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"codepoint-offset-block\", \"concept\": \"code-point arithmetic: constant offset K over every code point (Caesar on code points), text shifted into another Unicode block, XOR mask on code points\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Crib makes it keyless: K = ord(t[0]) - ord('N') (or from the first U+ token), verify K on the whole 87-char crib. Cover: generic +K (incl. K < 0 and K moving ASCII into CJK/PUA/astral), fullwidth (+0xFEE0, space -> U+3000), Tags (+0xE0000), enclosed/squared/negative-squared Latin (letter base per case, digits separate), math alphanumerics (per-style base table; NFKC handles most), XOR K on code points. Fixtures per block + U+ token form of a shifted text + decoys (ROT8000 must still route to rot8000).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"unicode-tag-smuggling\", \"concept\": \"invisible Unicode smuggling: Tags block (U+E0020-E007E) ASCII smuggling and variation-selector byte smuggling (U+FE00-FE0F, U+E0100-E01EF) after a carrier emoji\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). AI-arena-topical 'invisible instruction' encodings. tags_dec: keep chars in U+E0000-E007F -> chr(cp-0xE0000) (drop U+E0001 language tag / U+E007F cancel). vs_dec: VS1-16 -> 0-15, VS17-256 -> 16-255, bytes -> UTF-8 (Paul Butler emoji smuggling); ignore the visible carrier chars. Must read the artifact as UTF-8 (astral). Fixtures: tags after visible text, tags alone, emoji+VS, mixed with zero-width (zero-width decoder must not steal), decoys (real emoji sequences with VS16 U+FE0F only).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mixed-escape-soup\", \"concept\": \"every character in a randomly chosen notation (U+004E, \\\\u0069, &#99;, &#x65;, %20, \\\\x77, 0x6f, \\\\162, named entities)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Likely Time Trial / Mastery MUTATION of the D7/D8/D9/D25 codecs ('new unseen effects'). One alternation tokenizer over URL %xx, %uXXXX, HTML &#N; &#xH; &name;, \\\\uXXXX, \\\\UXXXXXXXX, \\\\u{..}, \\\\xNN, \\\\NNN octal, U+XXXX, 0xNN; whole text must tokenise (allow single separators); per-token radix. Prefer stdlib (html.unescape, unquote) only after splitting. Fixtures: uniform random mixtures, per-word notation, with and without separators; decoys: single-notation blobs (must give the same text).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"netstring-knobs\", \"concept\": \"netstring knobs: len > 1 and mixed lengths, len in UTF-8 bytes vs characters, leading zeros, whitespace/newlines between frames, missing final comma, nested netstrings, frames whose payload is a seen codec\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Most likely D25 knob on D24 'String Theory' (1-char netstrings). Generalise warmups_codecs.netstr_dec into netstr_any(t): cursor over BYTES (t.encode('utf-8')) and over chars, both tried; allow optional whitespace/newline between frames and a missing final ','; leading zeros in len; if every payload is itself a netstring run, recurse; if payloads are not English, run decode_chunk on each payload AND on the joined payload (frames of base64/hex/rot13). Whole text must parse or return None. Register in CANDIDATES + decode_chunk + as a free codec for 8-40 char Mastery fragments. Fixtures: len 1 / 2-9 / random mixed, UTF-8 multibyte payload (byte vs char), nested, b64-per-frame, decoys (plain prose with '3:' times).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"indexed-fragments-shuffled\", \"concept\": \"index-tagged frames out of order (`17:a,3:b`, `a@17`, `[17]a`) and run-count frames (`3:o,` = 'ooo')\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). 'Count carefully' re-read: the number before ':' may be a POSITION (frames shuffled; sort by index, check 0..n-1 or 1..n with no gaps/repeats) or a REPEAT COUNT (RLE: payload char x N). netstring len and count read the same when all N=1, so decide by N > 1 with a 1-char payload (count) or by a permutation of range(n) (index). Build frames_dec(t) trying netstring -> index-sort -> run-count, slug-gated. Fixtures: shuffled index frames (0- and 1-based, separators , ; space), RLE colon form 'N:c,' and 'c*N', decoys.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"tnetstrings\", \"concept\": \"tnetstrings (typed netstrings): `<len>:<data><tag>` with tags , string # int ^ float ! bool ~ null ] list } dict\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Sibling spec of D24 (Zed Shaw's typed netstrings). Recursive cursor parser: len, ':', len chars, one tag char; ']' / '}' payloads parse recursively; collect string leaves in order (and dict values) and join; slug-gate. Cue: netstring shape whose terminators vary over ',#^!~]}'. Fixtures: flat strings, a list of 1-char strings, a dict {i: char}, nested.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bencode-sexp\", \"concept\": \"bencode (`4:spam`, `i42e`, `l..e`, `d..e`) and Rivest canonical/transport S-expressions (`(3:abc5:hello)`, `[3:mime]`, `{base64}`, `|b64|`, `#hex#`)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Same 'len:data' atom as netstrings but NO trailing comma (BitTorrent bencode; SPKI canonical sexp). Cursor parser: digits ':' -> string of len; 'i'..'e' int; 'l'..'e' list; 'd'..'e' dict (key/value strings in order); sexp '(' ')' lists with optional '[display-hint]', transport forms {b64 of canonical} |b64 atom| #hex atom#. Join string leaves; slug-gate. Fixtures: bencoded list of 1-char strings, one long string, dict, canonical sexp, transport {..} form.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"length-prefix-binary-tlv\", \"concept\": \"binary length prefixes under hex/base64: Pascal strings, u16/u32 BE/LE lengths, varint (protobuf string field), TLV (type+len+value), hex-digit lengths\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Framing x the byte family (D19-D22 had byte-level knobs). After any hex/b64 decode, try: 1-byte len frames; 2/4-byte BE/LE len; LEB128 varint len (lib has LEB128); 1-byte type + 1/2-byte len (TLV); protobuf wire (tag byte 0x0a etc. + varint len); hex-digit len text frames ('0e' + data). Whole stream must parse; join payloads; slug-gate. Fixtures: one char per frame and one frame per word, each width/endianness, TLV with alternating type bytes, decoys (plain hex of text).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"case-and-digit-loss\", \"concept\": \"case-losing / digit-losing codecs (Morse, NATO, Polybius, tap, Bacon, A1Z26, T9)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Output must be lowercased; A1Z26/Polybius/Bacon/tap have no digit symbols, so slug digits are passed through literally or spelled - the decoder must keep non-alphabet tokens verbatim. Lowercase only the FINAL text, and test a collapsed output for an inner hex/digit layer first (D16 Morse -> UPPERCASE hex, D17 Bacon -> a-p).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"transposition-mastery-joint\", \"concept\": \"short Mastery fragments under a transposition (6-8 chars, key restarts per line)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D18 line 18 ('p-pr5e6' -> 'pper-56') has no charset cue; decode_chunked misses it (a D21 generalize worker's intermediate run left 'Master:i rbsk-...'). Keep the slug-gated per-line rail pass; register any future transposition decoder as a free codec with its day number so _joint_free tries it on each unclaimed line.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"affine-free-strict\", \"concept\": \"affine as a GM-confirmed-ready free codec\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Already prebuilt (wc:affine + SPECULATIVE_FREE + _fit_affine). Risk is only bigram ranking vs rot-N/Atbash in Mastery joint assignment; confirm ratchet path registers it with a day number on first sight.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"chepy-cyberchef-cue\", \"concept\": \"standard-tool fallback for any unseen encoding\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"chepy (venv) has from_base45/58/62/85/91/92/65536, rot_8000, from_upside_down, decode_zero_width, from_fullwidth, from_bacon, from_braille, from_nato, sms_decode_multitap, vigenere_decode, bifid_decode, gzip/zlib/bzip/lzma/lz4/lz77 _decompress, raw_inflate, unzip_all, tar_extract_all; CyberChef ops at tools/npm-cyberchef/node_modules/cyberchef/src/core/operations (Magic recipe finds compression layers).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"malbolge\", \"concept\": \"Malbolge programs (self-modifying, ternary crazy op)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Reference interpreter (~40 lines): memory 59049 trits-words, initialise from program (whitespace skipped, chars validated by (c + i) % 94 in the allowed set), fill rest with crazy(m[i-1], m[i-2]); ops by (c + i) % 94: 4 jmp, 5 out chr(a % 256), 23 in, 39 rotr, 40 mov d, 62 crazy, 68 nop, 81 end; encrypt with xlat2 after each step; step cap 10^7. Cue: printable soup starting '(=<`' / \\\"('&%\\\" with no b64/b85 structure. Fixtures: a known Malbolge program for a short text (generate with a vendored pure-python Malbolge generator if one fits in ~150 lines, else hand-include published hello-world style programs and accept fixtures for shorter strings).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"unicode-character-names\", \"concept\": \"Unicode character NAMES per character (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N, LATIN SMALL LETTER I, SPACE, ...`, `\\\\N{...}`)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Most likely D25 sequel ('every character has a point' -> 'has a name'). Decoder names_dec(t): split on , ; newline | or detect `\\\\N{..}`; each token -> unicodedata.lookup (case-insensitive, also aliases like 'HYPHEN-MINUS', 'FULL STOP', 'DIGIT FIVE'); whole text must resolve or None. Also accept `U+004E LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N` (UnicodeData.txt line style) and lowercase names. Register in CANDIDATES + decode_chunk + as a free codec for Mastery fragments (5-10 names per line). Fixtures: comma list, newline list, \\\\N{} string, UnicodeData lines, decoys (English prose in caps).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"codepoint-notation-knobs\", \"concept\": \"code-point notation knobs: `\\\\u{4E}`, `\\\\x{4E}`, short `U+4E`, lowercase `u+`, no separators, decimal/octal code points with prefixes, `\\\\N{U+004E}`\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). D26 knob on D25 (U+XXXX uppercase 4-hex, single spaces). Extend uplus_dec into codepoints_any(t): tokenizer `(?:[Uu]\\\\+|\\\\\\\\[uUx]\\\\{?|%u|0x|0d|0o|0b|&#x?|\\\\\\\\N\\\\{U\\\\+)([0-9A-Za-z]+)\\\\}?;?` with radix from the prefix; tokens may be adjacent (no separator) or separated by space , ; newline; whole text must tokenise. Fixtures: each prefix form, adjacency, astral (U+1F600), mixed case; decoys: plain hex, `0x` C arrays of BYTES (must still route to hex/char-code decoders: gate on slug).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"delta-encoded-codepoints\", \"concept\": \"delta / chained code points: first absolute then signed differences, running XOR, cumulative sums\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). 'Point to point' re-read. Integer token lists (decimal, signed, or U+/hex) whose values are mostly small and signed: try cumulative sum, XOR scan (c_i = v_i ^ c_{i-1}), and the reverse direction; chr; crib-gated (must START 'Nice work'). Fixtures: signed decimal deltas, hex deltas mod 256 / mod 0x110000, XOR chain hex, decoys (plain decimal char codes must not be re-summed).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"utf8-bytes-mojibake\", \"concept\": \"UTF-8 bytes vs code points: mojibake (UTF-8 read as cp1252/latin-1, double-encoded), byte escapes of multibyte sequences\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). 'Encoding confusion' sibling of D19-D20 and D25. Detect Ã/Â/ï¼/â€ runs; repair by t.encode('cp1252' or 'latin-1', errors='strict').decode('utf-8') repeated up to 3 times; then NFKC and the normal pipeline (fullwidth text survives as ASCII). Also `\\\\xef\\\\xbc\\\\xae` / `%EF%BC%AE` byte escapes -> bytes -> utf-8. ftfy not installed -- stdlib only. Fixtures: single/double mojibake of fullwidth and of plain text with smart quotes, cp1252 vs latin-1 differences (0x80-0x9F).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"script-letter-map-crib\", \"concept\": \"letter maps into another SCRIPT (runes, Greek, Cyrillic, Ogham, Deseret, Shavian, Tifinagh, Old Italic)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Non-Latin glyphs from one block with the crib's word lengths kept. First standard transliteration tables (Elder Futhark, Greek ISO 843-ish, Cyrillic GOST/ISO 9, Ogham, Deseret phonetic is NOT 1:1 -- keep as low), then generic crib mapping: glyph->letter from the 87-char crib (21 letters), unknown glyphs f j q y z resolved by the slug word list (adj-noun-noun from earlier days' slugs). Fixtures per script + a random-glyph-alphabet substitution.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"float-encoded-charcodes\", \"concept\": \"floating / fixed point numbers carrying char codes (IEEE-754 float32/64 hex, decimals 78.0, scaled fixed point, packed floats in base64)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). 'You Have a Point' read as decimal/floating point. Tokens 0x429c0000 (float32 BE of 78.0) / 16-hex doubles / '78.0' / '0.78' / '7.8e1'; base64 of packed '<f'/'>d' arrays. struct.unpack both endians, round, chr; fixed point: detect common scale 10^k or 2^k; crib-gated. Fixtures each + decoys (plain decimal codes).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"string-builder-literals\", \"concept\": \"'String' as code: concatenated string literals, String.fromCharCode / chr() calls, char arrays, ''.join([...]), JSON/Python lists of characters\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Title-pun sibling of D24 ('String Theory'). Never eval: regex/ast.literal_eval only on literal-only nodes. Forms: \\\"Ni\\\" + \\\"ce\\\" (JS/Java/C#), Python implicit 'N' 'i', String.fromCharCode(78,105,...), chr(78)+chr(105), new char[]{'N','i'}, [char]78+[char]105 (PowerShell), ''.join(['N','i']), [\\\"N\\\",\\\"i\\\"] JSON, b'\\\\x4e' bytes literals. Extract literal pieces in order, unescape, join; slug-gate. Overlaps lib.scripts deobfuscators -- reuse them. Fixtures per language + decoys.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"resp-redis\", \"concept\": \"RESP (Redis serialization protocol) bulk/simple strings and arrays\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Length-prefixed 'string protocol' sibling: '*<n>\\\\r\\\\n' arrays, '$<len>\\\\r\\\\n<data>\\\\r\\\\n' bulk strings, '+text\\\\r\\\\n', ':int\\\\r\\\\n', RESP3 '=' verbatim. Cursor parser tolerant to bare '\\\\n'; join bulk strings; slug-gate. Fixtures: array of 1-char bulk strings, one bulk string per word, CRLF vs LF.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"http-chunked-transfer\", \"concept\": \"HTTP/1.1 chunked transfer encoding as a text framing (hex chunk sizes, CRLF, final 0 chunk, chunk extensions)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Framing with HEX lengths ('Count carefully' in base 16). Parser: read hex size up to ';' or CRLF, take size bytes, expect CRLF, loop until size 0; tolerate LF-only and missing trailer. Optionally Content-Encoding gzip/deflate on the dechunked body (reuse magic sniff). Fixtures: 1-char chunks, word chunks, extensions, gzip body.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"asn1-der-tlv\", \"concept\": \"ASN.1 DER/BER TLV strings (UTF8String 0c, OCTET STRING 04, PrintableString 13, IA5String 16, SEQUENCE 30) in hex/base64/PEM\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Standard TLV framing. Minimal stdlib walker: tag byte, short/long-form length, recurse into constructed (0x20 bit) types, collect string leaves in order, join; slug-gate. Cues: hex '30 81'/'30 82'/'0c'+len, base64 'MI'/'MD'/'DA'. asn1crypto may not be importable in the solver -- keep it stdlib. Fixtures: SEQUENCE of 1-char UTF8Strings, one OCTET STRING, BER indefinite length.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"delimited-field-framing\", \"concept\": \"CSV/TSV quoted fields with doubled-quote escapes, Lisp char lists, YAML flow sequences carrying one char or word per field\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Delimiter-escaping sibling of netstrings (the payload contains the delimiter, escaped instead of counted). csv.reader for '\\\"N\\\",\\\"i\\\",\\\"\\\"\\\"\\\"' (doubled quote), TSV with \\\\t escapes, Scheme #\\\\N char literals, YAML flow [N, i, ' ']. Join fields; slug-gate. Fixtures each + decoys.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"figlet-ascii-art-text\", \"concept\": \"ASCII-art / dot-matrix rendering of the sentence (figlet banners, 5x7 bitmaps as #/. rows or hex rows)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Points'/'pixels' pun; less likely (the slug must be machine-readable). pyfiglet is NOT installed: vendor 2-3 standard figlet fonts (standard, banner, big) as glyph tables, segment glyphs by blank columns (or fixed width), match exactly; 5x7 dot-matrix: standard HD44780 font table, rows as #/. or hex. Fixtures: figlet standard of the sentence, banner font, 5x7 '#' rows, hex rows.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bifid-adfgvx\", \"concept\": \"Bifid / Trifid / ADFGX / ADFGVX / Nihilist\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"pycipher implements all; key from title; ADFGX cue is the 5/6-letter alphabet.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mime-encoded-word\", \"concept\": \"MIME encoded-word headers\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"email.header.decode_header handles =?charset?B|Q?...?=. Body-level MIME wrappers are in text-armor-wrappers.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"misc-tables\", \"concept\": \"chemical symbols, Cetacean, Citrix CTX1 (DNA/codon, Roman, BCD, Gray, Kenny are prebuilt in warmups_codecs)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Recognition cues only; each is a lookup table the worker can write in one turn; CyberChef has From BCD and Cetacean Cipher Decode.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"homoglyph\", \"concept\": \"homoglyph (Cyrillic/Greek look-alike) substitution\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"tool\", \"detail\": \"unidecode/confusables not installed; NFKC does not fold Cyrillic. Install unidecode only if a day uses it.\"}\n]\n```\n","numLines":120,"startLine":240,"totalLines":359}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018FFYr3MoSzBwdqNDSRAmmC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
